DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The pptthhrreeaadd__ccaanncceell() function sends a cancelation request to the thread
       _t_h_r_e_a_d.   Whether  and when the target thread reacts to the cancelation
       request depends on two attributes that are under the  control  of  that
       thread: its cancelability _s_t_a_t_e and _t_y_p_e.

       A   thread’s  cancelability  state,  determined  by  pptthhrreeaadd__sseettccaanncceell‐‐
       ssttaattee(3), can be _e_n_a_b_l_e_d (the default for new threads) or _d_i_s_a_b_l_e_d.  If
       a thread has disabled cancelation, then a cancelation  request  remains
       queued  until  the thread enables cancelation.  If a thread has enabled
       cancelation, then its cancelability type  determines  when  cancelation
       occurs.

       A  thread’s  cancelation  type, determined by pptthhrreeaadd__sseettccaanncceellttyyppee(3),
       may be either _a_s_y_n_c_h_r_o_n_o_u_s or _d_e_f_e_r_r_e_d (the default for  new  threads).
       Asynchronous cancelability means that the thread can be canceled at any
       time  (usually  immediately,  but  the system does not guarantee this).
       Deferred cancelability means that cancelation will be delayed until the
       thread next calls a function that is a _c_a_n_c_e_l_a_t_i_o_n _p_o_i_n_t.   A  list  of
       functions  that  are  or  may  be  cancelation  points  is  provided in
       pptthhrreeaaddss(7).

       When a cancelation request is acted on, the following steps  occur  for
       _t_h_r_e_a_d (in this order):

       (1)  Cancelation  clean‐up  handlers  are popped (in the reverse of the
            order   in   which   they   were   pushed)   and   called.    (See
            pptthhrreeaadd__cclleeaannuupp__ppuusshh(3).)

       (2)  Thread‐specific data destructors are called, in an unspecified or‐
            der.  (See pptthhrreeaadd__kkeeyy__ccrreeaattee(3).)

       (3)  The thread is terminated.  (See pptthhrreeaadd__eexxiitt(3).)

       The  above steps happen asynchronously with respect to the pptthhrreeaadd__ccaann‐‐
       cceell() call; the return status of pptthhrreeaadd__ccaanncceell()  merely  informs  the
       caller whether the cancelation request was successfully queued.

       After  a  canceled thread has terminated, a join with that thread using
       pptthhrreeaadd__jjooiinn(3) obtains PPTTHHRREEAADD__CCAANNCCEELLEEDD as the thread’s  exit  status.
       (Joining  with  a  thread  is the only way to know that cancelation has
       completed.)

